00:00:11.110 --> 00:00:13.110 align:start position:0%
an animated declaration of love  to monsieur hublot  a lonely bachelor whose daily existence  is organized down to the very last  detail  it's a world where everything is still  steam powered  where everything is mechanized there's  nothing organic in this world  it's all metal or leather  the challenge was to bring a bit of life  into this universe  it's the first time alexandra espiga has  directed a movie  he usually turns drawings into moving  images  and he's done animation work on such  international productions as happy feet  too  and star wars the clone wars tv series  as well as for the hollywood blockbuster  iron man 3.  i like the look from the start  i didn't know  as a character  he was only half baked when i came into  the project  so it wasn't so much his personality i  was drawn to at first as the world  itself  that world was created by the small salt  productions in luxembourg it's  specialized in 3d animation since 2007.  the group is currently working on a film  about an alien called wilfred whom a  computer program sends to earth by  accident  about a small mouse who has fantastic  adventures  but monsieur hublot is by far the  biggest and most successful production  for l'oreal vids  it started with a meeting with the  belgian sculptor stefano  i was already fascinated by his  characters i thought they told an  interesting story on paper and it would  be interesting to take it further with  animation  that was the beginning of the adventure  the adventure lasted three years and  involved some 50 people  every animated film begins with a  drawing and then each object is animated  individually to create a  three-dimensional image  these animated elements are then  combined to create a totality  animation means giving life to  characters  it's as if the animated characters were  real actors  you have to forget their 3d characters  are animated from paper and get the  impression they're alive  right in front of us  everyday life is also animated  and his life is changed forever by a  small whimpering dog  the 11 minute long short was a challenge  but not only in technical terms  the budget was 250 000 euros  that's a quarter fifth or a tenth of  what it would have taken to make the  film in optimal conditions  of course we had to struggle every day  to conclude the project  but it was worth it in the end  in march 2014 la rovitz and alexander  espigar were awarded the oscar for best  animated short film  when they called us up  i moved mechanically  that was probably the most  me  i got it mechanically took the oscar  speech  and then i looked at the stopwatch  and when 45 seconds had elapsed i said  it's time to leave the stage  the story of a man whose life is turned  upside down in 11 minutes has delighted  festival audiences around the world  earning the film not only in oscar but  countless other awards as well
 

